\b MyFps UE5.5 Technical Summary - Pickup, Drop, and Weapon Sync\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Goal\b0\par
This round focused on stabilizing multiplayer weapon interaction and dropped-weapon behavior. The concrete targets were:\par
- make pickup prompts visible only to the local overlapping player;\par
- make server-side pickup logic reliable in multiplayer;\par
- restore correct reload and tracer behavior after pickup;\par
- let dropped weapons fall to the ground first, then stop behaving like active physics bodies.\par
\par
\b 2. Root Problems\b0\par
Before the fixes, the project had several linked state issues:\par
- pickup prompt visibility was being driven directly by a world widget, so visibility could leak to non-local players;\par
- pickup interaction could succeed visually on a client while the authoritative server state did not fully accept the weapon handoff;\par
- dropped weapons were either fully physical for too long, causing character collision problems, or fully non-physical, causing weapons to hang in place instead of falling;\par
- when the authoritative held-weapon state did not settle cleanly on clients, downstream systems such as ammo refill on reload and tracer spawning also failed.\par
\par
\b 3. Pickup Prompt Strategy\b0\par
The pickup overlap flow in TP_PickUpComponent was split into two responsibilities:\par
- all instances still update AvailablePickup so the server has the correct pickup target when RPC logic runs;\par
- only locally controlled characters are allowed to show or hide the pickup prompt widget.\par
\par
This separation solved two problems at once:\par
- only the player who actually enters the pickup sphere sees Press E to pick up;\par
- the server-side character copy still knows which pickup is available, so authoritative pickup no longer fails because AvailablePickup is null.\par
\par
\b 4. Authority Model for Pickup and Drop\b0\par
Pickup and drop input in MyFpsCharacter was moved back to a server-authoritative path:\par
- client input sends ServerInteractPickup or ServerDropCurrentWeapon;\par
- the actual weapon ownership change happens on the server;\par
- the client no longer tries to perform the final state change locally first.\par
\par
This was important because client-first execution created split state. A client could appear to hold a weapon or play reload animation locally while the server-side weapon ownership and ammo state were not actually updated.\par
\par
\b 5. Weapon Collision State Model\b0\par
Weapon body collision was consolidated into a single state path inside TP_WeaponComponent:\par
- SetWeaponBodyCollisionEnabled;\par
- ApplyWeaponBodyCollisionState.\par
\par
Two stable modes were defined:\par
- enabled: short dropped-physics mode;\par
- disabled: static query-only mode.\par
\par
In static query-only mode, the weapon body:\par
- does not simulate physics;\par
- does not use gravity;\par
- ignores Pawn collision;\par
- still allows query behavior needed for traces and interaction support.\par
\par
This is what removed the earlier issue where a spawned or dropped weapon could physically interfere with characters and cause launch or fly-up behavior.\par
\par
\b 6. Two-Stage Dropped Weapon Behavior\b0\par
DropWeapon was converted from a single permanent state into a two-stage flow:\par
\par
\i Stage A: short physical drop\i0\par
- when the weapon is dropped, the server enables temporary body physics;\par
- the weapon uses SimulatePhysics(true), EnableGravity(true), and PhysicsOnly collision;\par
- this allows the weapon to visibly fall onto the ground.\par
\par
\i Stage B: settle into static interaction state\i0\par
- after a short timer defined by DroppedPhysicsDuration, the server calls FinishDroppedWeaponPhysics;\par
- the weapon returns to the non-physical query-only state.\par
\par
This design keeps the visual feel of a real drop while preventing long-lived physical collision from reintroducing player blocking or launch issues.\par
\par
\b 7. Restoring Client Weapon References After Replication\b0\par
OnRep_IsHeld in TP_WeaponComponent was extended so that when a held-weapon state replicates to a client:\par
- the component restores its owning Character reference;\par
- the client character updates CurrentWeaponComponent to this weapon;\par
- when the held state clears, the reference is removed again.\par
\par
This step is important because several gameplay systems depend on the character having a valid current weapon reference, including:\par
- reload state and ammo queries;\par
- local HUD ammo display;\par
- fire and tracer execution chains that depend on the correct equipped weapon component.\par
\par
\b 8. Observed Outcome\b0\par
After these changes, the following behaviors were restored and verified:\par
- pickup prompt visibility works at local-player scope;\par
- weapons can be picked up again in multiplayer;\par
- dropped weapons fall to the ground;\par
- reload can correctly refill ammo again;\par
- tracer effects can display again;\par
- the previous severe collision problem with dropped weapons was reduced by returning them to query-only state after the short drop window.\par
\par
\b 9. Remaining Direction\b0\par
At this point, the weapon interaction chain and dropped-weapon physics chain are in a much more stable state. Remaining issues should now be debugged on top of this stabilized base, especially weapon attachment presentation across first-person and third-person views.\par
